From 2c01605753f263f014a89f1c23b87d6a1c4ff054 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 15:02:19 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] ASoC: qcom: q6apm: tear down a graph whose PREPARE, START or
 STOP failed

When GRAPH_START times out, the DSP may have started the graph in part,
but q6apm keeps reusing it: STOP sends nothing, the next prepare sends
PREPARE and START again, and the final GRAPH_CLOSE result is ignored.
A speaker graph could stay silent until reboot.

Treat a failed PREPARE, START or STOP as leaving the graph in an
unknown state: mark it dead (refusing further commands), send STOP and
CLOSE, and drop it from graph_idr once the close is acknowledged so the
next open sends a fresh GRAPH_OPEN. An unacknowledged close leaves the
graph wedged with its id refused, until a late ack, a repeated close or
a DSP error for an unknown graph proves it closed. Graph state changes
take a per-graph mutex.

The LPASS BE and the FE prepare use q6apm_graph_is_dead() to close and
reopen a dead graph.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
